<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Quartz</RootNamespace>
    <Title>Quartz.NET TimeZoneConverter Integration</Title>
    <Description>Quartz.NET TimeZoneConverter integration - resolves a trigger's time zone by either its IANA or its Windows id, on either operating system, through https://github.com/mj1856/TimeZoneConverter; $(Description)</Description>
    <PackageTags>$(PackageTags);timezone;timezoneconverter;iana;tzdb</PackageTags>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>True</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <Authors>Sean Farrow, Marko Lahma, Quartz.NET</Authors>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <!--
      The claim, and what it is worth - the same statement Quartz.csproj makes, and read the same way.
      IsAotCompatible turns on IsTrimmable and the trim, AOT and single-file analyzers, all of which were
      already on here (issue #3431); what it adds is the statement, so the four are spelled out beside it.

      What is proven: Quartz.Trimming.Canary references this package, is published by ILCompiler as a native
      executable on Windows, Linux and macOS on every pull request, and is run - without ICU, which is the
      one shape in which this package can be seen to do anything. It asserts that .NET alone refuses an
      IANA id on Windows and a Windows id elsewhere, then that TimeZones.FindById answers it through the
      resolver UseTimeZoneConverter registered, and schedules a trigger in the zone and reads it back.

      What a native publish reports against this package: nothing, and nothing was ever reported.
      UseTimeZoneConverter registers one resolver with TimeZones and that resolver asks TZConvert for a
      TimeZoneInfo by its IANA or Windows id, which is data rather than a type name. So there is no
      TrimAnalysisBaseline.cs beside this file, and a warning appearing here is a new one - fix it rather
      than starting a baseline for it; build/Build.cs fails the canary leg on one, since there is no file
      for it to be recorded in.

      Where it stops: the claim is this assembly's. TimeZoneConverter underneath it carries its mapping as
      data and says nothing about trimming of its own.
    -->
    <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>
    <IsTrimmable>true</IsTrimmable>
    <EnableTrimAnalyzer>true</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
    <EnableAotAnalyzer>true</EnableAotAnalyzer>
    <EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>true</EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="TimeZoneConverter" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Quartz\Quartz.csproj" />

  </ItemGroup>
</Project>